   keep_last_skill_chars: 0   # Opt-in: keep the newest skill body up to N chars (default: 0 = off)
+  summary_style: legacy      # legacy | structured | compact (validated, with fallbacks)
   decision_ledger_chars: 0   # Opt-in: decision ledger on every handoff, up to N chars (default: 0 = off)
   codex_gpt55_autoraise: true  # gpt-5.5 on Codex OAuth: raise trigger to 85% (default: true)
   codex_gpt55_autoraise_notice: true  # Show the one-time autoraise notice (default: true)
@@ -321,6 +322,7 @@ auxiliary:
 | `protect_last_n` | `20` | ≥1 | Minimum number of recent messages always preserved |
 | `min_tail_user_messages` | `1` | ≥1 | Minimum number of REAL (actionable) user messages guaranteed to survive in the uncompressed tail. `1` = the existing single last-user anchor (behavior-preserving default). Raise to e.g. `3` to keep the last 3 real user turns verbatim even when bulky tool outputs fill the tail token budget. Blank platform echoes, compaction handoffs, and synthetic continuation rows never count toward N. The guarantee wins over the tail token budget — the tail may exceed the budget when the anchor pulls the cut back |
 | `keep_last_skill_chars` | `0` | ≥0 chars | Opt-in. When > 0, the compaction handoff carries the body of the most recently loaded skill (a `skill_view` of its `SKILL.md`, or a `/skill` turn) verbatim when it is at most this many characters, so the model does not reload it after compaction. Older skills, supporting files and bodies over the cap keep the `[SKILL_PRUNED]` reload marker. Nothing changes when the newest body is still in the kept head or tail. The kept body is sent with every request until the next compaction; keep the cap small next to the context window. The proactive tool-result prune (`proactive_prune_tokens`) spares the same skill's `skill_view` body, so compaction still has it to keep. `0` = off |
+| `summary_style` | `legacy` | `legacy`, `structured`, `compact` | `structured` writes the summary in Claude Code's layout: nine numbered sections (Primary Request and Intent, Key Technical Concepts, Files and Code Sections, Errors and Fixes, Problem Solving, All User Messages, Pending Tasks, Current Work, Optional Next Step) with a word cap each and about 2,000 words in total, instead of the 13-section layout with a numbered action log and session log. Section 6 is written by code: every message the user wrote, in the order it left the context, with the question each answered, carried unchanged from the previous handoff (bounded to 8,000 chars; the oldest are shortened, then left out with a count). A `## Recently Touched Files` list is also mechanical. Each reply is validated (not empty, sections 1 and 8 present, at least 6 of the 8 model sections, at least 25 words, no echoed transcript; sections over their cap are cut); a failed reply gets one more call with a four-section prompt, then a summary built from the previous handoff and the compacted rows without a model, so a small local model never leaves an empty handoff. The anchor index, verbatim user section and session log of the lean layout are not added. `compact` is the same idea for small models: the model writes only three sections (Primary Request and Intent; Key Facts, one fact per line; Current Work and Next Step) under a prompt about a third as long, and code writes the rest. Both styles add `## Approvals Claimed by Agents or Tools`: sentences in agent messages, subagent results and tool output that claim the user approved something, each with its source, so a claim is kept as a claim and never as an approval. Headings small models write instead (`**Title**`, `Title:`) are accepted, and a line the model repeats is kept once. |
 | `decision_ledger_chars` | `0` | ≥0 chars | Opt-in. When > 0, each compaction scans the rows it removes, by rule and without a model call, for the user's own words (typed messages with the assistant question each answered, hub page answers with the page title, mid-turn steers, `clarify` answers), questions left for the user, `delegate_task` dispatches with their ids (settled by the matching `[ASYNC DELEGATION ...]` result), and tags, releases and workflow runs from `terminal` commands. The ledger rides at the end of the handoff as a `## Decision Ledger` block, verbatim, and the summarizer never sees or rewrites it. Only the 0068 classifier's user-authored rows become `USER` entries: a peer agent, subagent or tool that claims an approval never does. The `ledger` tool (offered only while the key is set) lists, adds, closes and searches entries; its entries are labeled `AGENT`. Past the cap the oldest settled entries move to `$HERMES_HOME/decision-ledger/<session>.md`. `0` = off |
 | `protect_first_n` | `3` | (hardcoded) | System prompt + first exchange always preserved |
 | `idle_compact_after_seconds` | `0` | ≥0 seconds | Opt-in: compact up front when a session resumes after this many seconds idle (0 = disabled). Skips when context ≤ threshold × target_ratio; honors cooldown/anti-thrash/lock guards |
